ne** describes an inside position, which is not the relation specified in this question. The requested location is at the finish line. Into describes movement, not the static relation requested here.","D":"**on the finish line** describes contact with a surface, which is not the relation specified in this question. The requested location is at the finish line. Into describes movement, not the static relation requested here."}}},{"id":"2a7fb912-68b2-5740-a1e2-aa10f6aa8912","topicSlug":"prepositions-of-place","level":"A1","prompt":"In “a race official at the finish line”, with the meaning “treating the line as a designated race point”, what relation does at express?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"In contact with a surface"},{"id":"B","text":"Movement into a space"},{"id":"C","text":"At a conventional activity location or a point"},{"id":"D","text":"Within a space or container"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"At often marks an exact point or an activity location. Expressions such as at home and at work are conventional. Some places allow different prepositions with different meanings; each question specifies its intended meaning. Focus on **spatial meaning** in this question.","explanation":"Here, at describes treating the line as a designated race point. The same place can take another preposition if its meaning changes.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"spatial-meaning","contextKey":"place-50","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-prepositions-of-place"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v2","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"ba80dfe5e3a0d508505a76fde888804f1fc0c5fc64c4578555e914e664f67ab1","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **spatial meaning**. At often marks an exact point or an activity location. Expressions such as at home and at work are conventional. Some places allow different prepositions with different meanings; each question specifies its intended meaning.","Here, at describes treating the line as a designated race point. The same place can take another preposition if its meaning changes.","Choose **At a conventional activity location or a point**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"**In contact with a surface** describes an inside position, which is not the relation specified in this question. Here, at describes treating the line as a designated race point. The same place can take another preposition if its meaning changes.","B":"**Movement into a space** describes movement into a space, which is not the relation specified in this question. Here, at describes treating the line as a designated race point. The same place can take another preposition if its meaning changes.","C":"Here, at describes treating the line as a designated race point. The same place can take another preposition if its meaning changes.","D":"**Within a space or container** describes an inside position, which is not the relation specified in this question. Here, at describes treating the line as a designated race point. The same place can take another preposition if its meaning changes."}}},{"id":"8817d32f-c87d-5dea-af9d-d58c14c5c74a","topicSlug":"prepositions-of-place","level":"A1","prompt":"Replace “into” to state a static location: “A race official is into the finish line.” Intended meaning: treating the line as a designated race point.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"at"},{"id":"B","text":"in"},{"id":"C","text":"on"},{"id":"D","text":"into"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"At often marks an exact point or an activity location. Expressions such as at home and at work are conventional. Some places allow different prepositions with different meanings; each question specifies its intended meaning. Focus on **static correction** in this question.","explanation":"For the stated static meaning, replace into with at: a race official is at the finish line.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"static-correction","contextKey":"place-50","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-prepositions-of-place"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v2","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"aa31a57c12aee83fee374746563293d050fdb1de8e10328dea9ca0189940cd4e","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **static correction**. At often marks an exact point or an activity location. Expressions such as at home and at work are conventional. Some places allow different prepositions with different meanings; each question specifies its intended meaning.","For the stated static meaning, replace into with at: a race official is at the finish line.","Choose **at**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"For the stated static meaning, replace into with at: a race official is at the finish line.","B":"**in** describes an inside position, which is not the relation specified in this question. For the stated static meaning, replace into with at: a race official is at the finish line.","C":"**on** describes contact with a surface, which is not the relation specified in this question. For the stated static meaning, replace into with at: a race official is at the finish line.","D":"**into** describes movement into a space, which is not the relation specified in this question. For the stated static meaning, replace into with at: a race official is at the finish line."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Place prepositions describe a relation, not a permanent property of a location. The same place can be presented as a container, a surface or a point in a route. The prompt’s intended relation is therefore essential; a dictionary list of place names alone is not enough."],"rules":[{"title":"Inside a space","explanation":"In locates something within a container, area, city or country. Read the stated physical relation carefully.","examples":["The tools are in a chest.","The village is in a valley.","The picture is **on** the wall."],"pattern":"**in** a room / **in** a city","paragraphs":["In commonly presents a place as an enclosing space or an area within boundaries. A person can be in a room or in a country. The enclosure need not be a physical box. Into instead highlights movement from outside to inside; it is not the ordinary static counterpart of in."]},{"title":"On a surface","explanation":"On describes contact with a surface, including vertical surfaces. It does not simply mean higher than something.","examples":["The chart is on the wall.","The cloth is on the counter.","The keys are **in** the drawer."],"pattern":"**on** a shelf / **on** the ceiling","paragraphs":["On commonly describes contact with a surface, even a vertical or overhead one: on the wall, on the ceiling. It also appears in conventional phrases such as on a bus. Surface contact is a useful core meaning, but not every use is literal."]},{"title":"At a point or activity","explanation":"At often marks an exact point or an activity location. Expressions such as at home and at work are conventional. Some places allow different prepositions with different meanings; each question specifies its intended meaning.","examples":["Meet me at the entrance.","She is at work.","Meet me **at** the ticket barrier."],"pattern":"**at** the entrance / **at** work","paragraphs":["At often presents a location as a point, meeting place or conventional activity location. At school can mean attending school; in the school foregrounds being inside the building. At the desk does not mean standing on its surface."]},{"title":"Contrasts and edge cases","explanation":"Go to gives a destination, go into gives entry and come out of gives exit. Across goes from one side to another, through follows an interior passage, and along follows a length. Home often works without to after go. Prepositions vary with conventional usage and sometimes with national variety.","example